Antiphospholipid Syndrome
Haematology & Oncology
Unprovoked recurrent DVT/PE, Recurrent unexplained pregnancy loss, Livedo reticularis, Persistent specific antiphospholipid antibodies

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-005
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of pleuritic chest pain and dyspnoea in a high-risk patient requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute pulmonary embolism.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young female with a history of recurrent miscarriages presenting with acute, unprovoked pleuritic chest pain. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to stratify the immediate risk of a pulmonary embolism, which carries a high overnight mortality risk if left unassessed until regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have this sharp pain in my ribs when I take a deep breath, and I noticed this weird purple lacy rash on my legs again."
History of Present Illness: The 32-year-old patient reports a sudden onset of right-sided pleuritic chest pain that began 4 hours ago. The pain is sharp, exacerbated by deep inspiration, and accompanied by mild dyspnoea. She denies cough or fever. She reports a history of two unexplained first-trimester miscarriages and a previous unprovoked DVT in her left leg 3 years ago. She has also suffered from severe persistent migraine-like headaches recently.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently resting to avoid exacerbating the chest pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential recurrence of blood clots.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Anxious young adult. Breathing shallowly to avoid pleuritic pain.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. No murmurs or gallops.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No pleural rub appreciated, but splinting on right-sided deep inspiration is noted.
Extremities: A persistent, non-blanching purplish reticular rash (livedo reticularis) is visible on bilateral thighs and forearms. No unilateral calf swelling, erythema, or tenderness today.
Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. No focal deficits or signs of chorea.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean home. No immediate environmental hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently taking paracetamol for migraines; no prophylactic anticoagulation prescribed previously.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Antiphospholipid Syndrome (Suspected Pulmonary Embolism) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Musculoskeletal Chest Pain (Costochondritis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: lack of chest wall tenderness on palpation and the presence of tachycardia with a high-risk clinical history.

Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of fever, productive cough, and clear lung fields on auscultation.

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: bilateral equal air entry, normal percussion note, and stable oxygen saturations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of pleuritic chest pain and tachycardia in a young female with a history of unprovoked DVT, recurrent miscarriages, and the clinical sign of livedo reticularis creates a very high pre-test probability for a new pulmonary embolism secondary to underlying Antiphospholipid Syndrome. Delaying assessment until the morning hours would expose the patient to the unacceptable risk of massive pulmonary embolism, haemodynamic collapse, and death. This presentation meets the strict criteria for an urgent after-hours assessment to clinically evaluate for acute VTE and arrange immediate escalation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for a CT Pulmonary Angiogram (CTPA) to rule out PE, along with a coagulation profile and specific antiphospholipid antibody screening (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, anti-beta-2-glycoprotein I).
Positive Behavioral Change:

Avoid deep, forceful breathing or unnecessary exertion while waiting for transport. By keeping your respiratory rate and heart rate as stable as possible, you minimize stress on the right side of your heart.

Do not massage your legs or take any sedating medications tonight. By avoiding leg manipulation, you prevent dislodging any potential further clots.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED transfer is mandated. Call 000 for an ambulance if you experience sudden extreme breathlessness, coughing up blood, severe dizziness, or fainting.

